{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Not like the land of Egypt it is.</b> For it depends on irrigation, as it says, ״והשקית ברגליך כגן הירק״ — and you would water it with your foot, like a vegetable garden. The land is not, in this respect, dependent on human toil, but rather ״למטר השמים תשתה מים״ — it drinks water from the rain of heaven. Therefore you will need the fulfillment of the Torah: if you fulfill the Torah and the commandments, the heavens will give their dew and the earth will give its yield; and if not, ״ועצר את השמים ולא יהיה מטר והאדמה לא תתן את יבולה״ — and He will shut up the heavens and there will be no rain, and the ground will not give its yield, as it says in the parashah adjacent to this one. And even though, had it been a land irrigated like the land of Egypt, it would have been easy for HaShem to dry up the waters for them and to destroy the rivers if they did not fulfill the Torah, the Ramban, of blessed memory, already wrote concerning this that this passage warns according to the way of the world: the land, inasmuch as it is mountains and valleys, is destroyed more quickly than other lands when He does not send the rains of His might there, for the sick person needs merit and prayer to heal him more than the healthy person who is not afflicted with illness."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:24.188339390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}